6. SOURCE VERIFICATION AND TRACEABILITY
This is one of the most important requirements.
Every extracted curriculum element should retain source information wherever possible.
Examples:

Subject: Page 3.
Module 1: Page 4.
Topic: Page 5.
Assessment scheme: Page 12.
PPTX slide: Slide 7.

Each extracted item must distinguish between:
A. EXPLICIT
Directly found in the uploaded document.
B. INFERRED
Derived from document structure or context but not explicitly stated.
C. AI_SUPPLEMENTARY
Suggested by Gemini to improve learning but not present in the original source.
Never misrepresent AI_SUPPLEMENTARY material as official syllabus content.
Confidence
Support:

HIGH.
MEDIUM.
LOW.
INSUFFICIENT.

Confidence must reflect extraction quality and evidence, not arbitrary certainty.
Examples:

Clear printed heading with page reference: potentially HIGH.
Ambiguous scanned heading: MEDIUM or LOW.
Incomplete or unreadable content: INSUFFICIENT.

A confidence score must not replace the actual source reference.
Verification warnings
Detect and display:

Missing pages.
Unreadable text.
Uncertain topic boundaries.
Possible duplicate subjects.
Inconsistent semester numbering.
Missing module headings.
Conflicting information between documents.
Ambiguous academic year.
Uncertain institution identification.

If two documents conflict, show the conflicting values and ask the user to resolve them.
Do not silently select one.

